{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Finding (audit of epic 3405-c0b7)\n**Site:** `src/rebar/_reads.py:99` sets `err.error_code = \"pinned_ticket_read_failed\"`, returned verbatim by `error_code_for` branch 5 (the `exc.error_code` passthrough). But `pinned_ticket_read_failed` is NOT a member of `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES` (`src/rebar/_errors.py:48-66`).\n\n**Problem:** the code itself is descriptive (NOT misleading). The defect is a vocabulary-completeness gap: `error_code_for`'s own docstring says it \"Returns a stable vocabulary code (one of KNOWN_ERROR_CODES)\", but this passthrough emits a code outside that set. Any consumer that validates the returned code against `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES` (the documented contract) would reject a legitimately-emitted code, or fall back to treating it as unknown.\n\n**Observable vs latent:** OBSERVABLE — emitted on the pinned-ticket read failure path; reaches the MCP envelope guard and the CLI reader envelope.\n\n**Proposed fix — READY-TO-FIX (reuse existing, no design decision):** add `\"pinned_ticket_read_failed\"` to the `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES` frozenset so the emitted vocabulary and the declared vocabulary agree. (Alternatively, add a contract test that asserts every `err.error_code` string assigned in the tree is a member of `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES` — see the epic's prevention recommendation.) Low priority; not misleading, but it violates the stated vocabulary contract.\n\n**caused_by / class:** vocabulary drift, not the `llm_unavailable` pattern.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] `pinned_ticket_read_failed` is a member of the `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES` frozenset in `src/rebar/_errors.py`, so the emitted vocabulary and the declared vocabulary agree.\n- [x] The `pinned_ticket_read_failed` code emitted on the pinned-ticket read-failure path is proven to be a `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES` member through the real read surface by the held-out oracle `tests/interfaces/contracts/test_pinned_ticket_read_failed_0aee.py`.\n- [x] The public-surface drift gate stays green — the pinned constant `rebar.KNOWN_ERROR_CODES` gains the new member in the committed baseline `tests/unit/api_surface_baseline.json`; checked: `python scripts/gen_api_surface.py --check` exits 0.\n\n## Testing\n- `python -m pytest tests/interfaces/contracts/test_pinned_ticket_read_failed_0aee.py -q`\n- `python -m pytest tests/unit/test_api_surface_gate.py -q`\n- `python scripts/gen_api_surface.py --check`"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788077028493787001,"uuid":"e4a41680-ab56-43b5-9889-7be29d73a95d"}